WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.760
Okay, I'm ready.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.660
The recently released Census report on poverty in 2017 shows some positive developments.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.000
The official poverty rate declined from 12.7 to 12.3 percent,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.140
meaning that almost a million fewer Americans
were in poverty last year than the year before.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:26.840
Income from earnings increased, which helped minority groups reach their lowest poverty rates ever.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.820
But a word of caution: We shouldn't put too much stock

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.260
in the official poverty rate because it doesn't count many of the tax and transfer programs

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.360
that help low-income Americans.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.720
To see how well the US is doing in fighting material deprivation,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.600
we should pay more attention to consumption poverty, a metric that tells us

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.740
what low-income Americans have and can afford.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.620
One way in which the official poverty rate is helpful, though, is that it tells us

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.900
how many Americans say they are earning their way out of poverty through work.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:00.080
A small decline like we saw is definitely a good thing, but it's not enough.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:07.280
Let's hope we see more Americans working and earning their way out of poverty in the 2018 data.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.080
What do you think about the way we measure poverty in the United States?

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.980
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:17.520
Also let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.220
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

